      "prologue": {
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "morning",
            "text": "Eli woke to the sound of rain needling the roof of the cottage, a steady drum on the cedar shakes that meant the morning chores would take twice as long. He lay still for a moment under the quilt his mother had patched with scraps of old linen, listening to the gutters gurgle and spill. The room smelled of dried yarrow and the faint, clean sharpness of the elixir vial he’d left uncorked on the nightstand—just for a breath, just to see the soft green pulse of it in the dark before he’d fallen asleep. He reached for it now, his fingers finding the familiar cool glass, and tucked it into the inner pocket of his vest before swinging his legs over the side of the bed.\n\nIn the kitchen, he lit the stove and set the kettle on the iron grate. The fire caught slowly, the kindling still damp from the wet that had seeped in through the poorly sealed window frame. Eli crouched and blew gently until the flames steadied, then straightened and rubbed the ache from his knees. His mother’s porridge bowl sat rinsed and upturned on the drying rack; she’d already gone to the library, leaving the day’s list pinned under a smooth stone on the table. *Woundwort. Elder bark. Check the snares on the east ridge.* He read it twice, though he’d known what it would say before he looked.\n\nHe ate standing up, spooning the oats without tasting them, his eyes on the window where the rain blurred the garden into smears of green and brown. The garden needed weeding. The goat needed milking. The morning stretched ahead of him in a familiar chain of small, necessary things, and for a little while he let it be enough. He scraped the bowl clean, pulled on his oiled cloak, and stepped out into the wet. The path to the shed was mud already, sucking at his boots, and the goat bleated at him from the lean-to with the particular irritation of an animal who did not care for rain."
          },
          {
            "title": "the people",
            "text": "The forest floor was softer than the garden path, matted with old needles and the wet pulp of last autumn’s leaves. Eli moved slowly along the deer trail, the basket slung over his shoulder already half-full with woundwort and the pale, papery curls of elder bark. He’d checked the snares first: one empty, one sprung by something that had chewed itself free, leaving only a tuft of grey fur matted with blood. He reset the trap and wiped his fingers on his trousers before handling the plants. The oiled cloth he wrapped them in was worn thin at the folds, and he made a mental note to ask his mother for wax next time she traded at the chandlery.\n\nBy the time he reached the library, the rain had eased to a drizzle that clung to his hair in fine droplets. The building hunched at the edge of the village, a squat stone structure with a door that stuck in the damp. Eli leaned his shoulder against it and pushed until it gave. Inside, the air was dry and still, thick with the smell of old paper and the faint, green sharpness of the drying herbs his mother hung from the rafters. Mara was at her desk by the window, a heavy tome open before her and a half-mended binding laid across a scrap of linen. She didn’t look up when he entered, but her hand stilled on the needle.\n\n“You’re wet,” she said.\n\n“It’s raining.”\n\n“The cloak is for rain, Eli.”\n\nHe hung it on the hook by the door, where it dripped onto the flagstones in a slow, steady rhythm. “The snares were empty. Well, almost. I brought what I could.”\n\nHe set the basket on the corner of her desk, careful not to disturb the pages. Mara glanced at the contents, then at him. Her eyes were the same pale grey as the sky outside, and they took him in the way they always did, reading more than he meant to offer. She wore her hair pinned back simply, the same way she had for as long as he could remember, and there was a smudge of ink on her jaw.\n\n“You ate,” she said. It wasn’t a question, but he nodded anyway. She seemed satisfied. She reached into the basket and lifted a sprig of woundwort, turning it in her thin fingers. “Good. Mrs. Alton’s boy burned his arm on the hearth. I’ll send this up with one of the salves.” She set it aside and picked up her needle again. “There’s bread and cheese in the back if you want it.”\n\n“Later.” Eli pulled the stool from under the counter and sat, his back against the cool stone wall. For a moment there was only the soft scratch of her needle through the binding, the rustle of a page she turned with the heel of her hand. It was an old rhythm between them, a quiet he’d known since he was small enough to sit under her desk and sort the scraps of parchment.\n\n“The goat’s stall needs mucking out,” Mara said without looking up. “I noticed this morning.”\n\n“I’ll do it before supper.”\n\nShe nodded, and the conversation settled into the space between them, complete and comfortable. Outside, the rain began again, a gentle hush against the window glass."
          },
          {
            "title": "the weight",
            "text": "Eli reached into his pocket and his fingers found the small glass vial, smooth and warm from his own heat. Inside it, the elixir gave off a faint amber glow, barely visible in the daylight that leaked through the rain-streaked window. He rolled it between his thumb and forefinger, a habit he couldn’t seem to break. The cork was still tight. The liquid still held its light.\n\n“I’ll need to go out again this evening,” he said.\n\nMara’s needle paused. Only for a breath, then it resumed its steady path through the spine of the book in her lap. “You went out last night.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“And the night before that.”\n\nHe didn’t answer. She didn’t look at him, but he felt the weight of her attention all the same, the way she saw the shadows under his eyes and the mud still caked on the heels of his boots. She would have noticed those boots by the door this morning, still damp, the laces stiff with dried muck from the grove.\n\n“Mrs. Alton’s salve is due by noon tomorrow,” she said. “And I’ve six more bindings to finish for the magistrate before the end of the week. If you’re gone, that falls to me.”\n\n“I won’t be long.” He pulled the vial from his pocket and set it on his knee, watching the light pool inside the glass. “There’s a patch of feverfew I’ve been meaning to check, out past the mill. If it’s ready to harvest, we’ll have enough to see the whole village through winter.”\n\nMara tied off a thread and snipped it clean with the small scissors she kept on a chain around her neck. “Feverfew,” she said. “At night.”\n\n“It’s easier to spot the healthy plants by the glow of the moss. You know that.”\n\nShe did know that. She’d taught him that. But she also knew the path to the mill didn’t take two hours in the dark, and it didn’t leave a boy’s hands smelling of old ash and crushed glass. She set the book aside and looked at him full now, her grey eyes steady, and for a long moment neither of them spoke. The rain tapped against the glass like a finger asking to be let in."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "intended_ending": "Eli learns his father did not die or abandon them — years ago his father bound his own life into the land to slow the kingdom's magic from dying out; the failing 'dying light' IS his father's fading life, spent to keep everyone else alive. Eli's mother has known all along and hid it, terrified the Crown would do the same to her son. The story drives toward Eli's choice: free his father (and let the kingdom die faster) or take his father's place alone. He reaches a THIRD answer earned by his arc (learning he doesn't have to carry it alone): he ties the burden across the last handful of mages and the people themselves, breaking the cycle of one vanished man sacrificing everything — bittersweet, his father finally allowed to die, the light dimmer but shared.",
